id: decisions/045-rule-versus-carve-out
title: "Every rule with violations keeps a shrink-only ledger"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'rule-versus-carve-out'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: rule-versus-carve-out
axis_gloss: "whether one rule applies to everything without exception, or known violations are enumerated in a named, shrinking exception list"
poles:
  a: explicit-grandfather-ledger
  b: uniform-rule
split: lone-exception
draft_status: full
leverage: 4.2
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(7) x split_weight(0.4) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  When a rule has known violations, should every such rule carry a named,
  shrink-only exception list the way the epistemic-status test does, or should a
  rule with exceptions not be called a rule yet?

why_it_matters: >
  One rule in the tree (the seven-value epistemic vocabulary) is enforced with a
  frozen, file-keyed ledger that a test caps at five and that may only shrink;
  three others (every active folder carries node.yawn, extensions are lowercase,
  names are kebab-case) have thirteen, seven and three known violations with no
  ledger and validators that cannot see them. A bot inherits whether a rule it
  breaks gets named or gets ignored; a child Yawn learns whether history is
  rewritten or listed.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    6 judgments sit at explicit-grandfather-ledger (3 authored) and 1 at
    uniform-rule (1 authored). The repository asserts both directions on this
    axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN, a folder on Dave's own disk outside the
    repository, is to be renamed lowercase or permanently ledgered; the
    invariant's source-path clause reaches outside the repo and only Dave can act
    there.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      Every rule with existing violations names them in a shrink-only, file-keyed
      ledger with a size ceiling asserted by test. A violation not in the ledger
      fails the build. The ledger may only shrink; a fix removes its entry.
    cost: "Three new tests in the shape of tests/epistemic-status-vocabulary.test.mjs (folder-node, extension case, kebab naming); ledgers of thirteen folders, six files and three files respectively; scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs taught to check extension case."
    risk: "Ledgers that never shrink become a second rulebook; the ceiling test is what prevents it and must ship with each ledger."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Copy the three-test shape (scan, template, shrink-only ceiling) from tests/epistemic-status-vocabulary.test.mjs to tests/folder-node.test.mjs, tests/canonical-extension-case.test.mjs and tests/naming.test.mjs; add a case check to scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0324"
      - "J0625"
      - "J0908"
      - "J0988"
      - "J1001"
      - "J1178"
  - name: B
    description: >
      A rule is stated only when its violations are zero. Until then it is a
      proposal and no test enforces it.
    cost: "Demoting the core/folder-node.yawn rule, canonical-extension invariant[0] and the naming convention to proposals; the epistemic-status ledger deleted and the five records rewritten."
    risk: "History rewritten silently, which the test comment names as the thing being prevented; three real rules lose their teeth while waiting for zero."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "None; B is the absence of ledgers."
    evidence_for:
      - "J1179"
  - name: C
    description: >
      Option A, with three additions: every ledger entry carries a reason and a
      date; all ledgers live either in the enforcing test or in one
      records/grandfather-ledgers.yawn that the tests read; and a violation the
      validator could not see is added to the ledger the day it is found, never left
      invisible. The MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN workspace path is the first such entry.
    cost: "Same as A plus a reason and date per entry and one records file."
    risk: "A ledger in a records file can be edited without a test noticing unless the test reads it; keep the ceiling in the test."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Same as A; each test reads its ledger (inline or from the records file) and asserts size is at most the count on the day it was written."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0625"
      - "J0908"
      - "J1001"
      - "J1178"
      - "J1179"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.85
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The ledger pattern has three authored and four proof-attached judgments, and
    the only judgment the extraction placed opposite (J1179, the size ceiling) is
    the constraint that makes the pattern safe, not an argument against it; both
    live in the same test file. GROUND_TRUTH FACTS 4, 8 and 20 show three stated
    rules with unledgered violations, and FACT 20 shows the worst case: the
    lowercase invariant is violated by the workspace root of four scheduled bots
    and the validator cannot see it. C generalizes the one executable pattern the
    repo already trusts and closes the invisible-violation gap.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "tests/ holds a scan-plus-ceiling test for folder-node, extension case and kebab naming, each with a frozen ledger, all passing"
    - "scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs checks extension case, and MASTER-CONTROL.YAWN is either renamed or ledgered with a reason"
    - "Every ledger entry carries a reason and a date"
    - "No ledger ceiling has been raised in any commit"

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A ledger grows (its ceiling is raised) in a commit, or a violation is cleared
    by editing the rule's wording instead of the record.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0324"
  - "J0625"
  - "J0908"
  - "J0988"
  - "J1001"
  - "J1178"
  - "J1179"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
